WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:06.722
Today you count all the way to twenty.

2
00:00:06.942 --> 00:00:11.586
You will count carefully, read the
numbers, and find odd and even.

3
00:00:12.086 --> 00:00:14.872
Counting is saying one
number for each thing.

4
00:00:15.092 --> 00:00:17.832
Touch each one once. One. Two. Three.

5
00:00:18.052 --> 00:00:20.467
Never say two numbers for the same thing.

6
00:00:20.687 --> 00:00:23.799
Keep going to the end of
the frame. That is ten.

7
00:00:24.019 --> 00:00:28.291
Now mix them up. Nothing came
or went, so it is still ten.

8
00:00:28.511 --> 00:00:33.434
Take them all away. An empty frame
is zero, and zero is a number.

9
00:00:33.934 --> 00:00:37.695
You do not always have to count.
Some patterns you just see.

10
00:00:37.915 --> 00:00:42.188
Five dots, like a dice. You
know it is five at a glance.

11
00:00:42.408 --> 00:00:46.402
Four and two make six. You
did not count one by one.

12
00:00:46.622 --> 00:00:51.591
Look at these apples. An estimate is
a sensible guess before you count.

13
00:00:51.811 --> 00:00:56.223
Guess about fifteen. Then count
them: fourteen. Very close.

14
00:00:56.723 --> 00:00:59.323
Fill the first frame
right up. That is ten.

15
00:00:59.543 --> 00:01:03.351
After ten we do not start
again. We keep the ten.

16
00:01:03.571 --> 00:01:07.101
Ten and one more is eleven.
Ten and two is twelve.

17
00:01:07.321 --> 00:01:10.897
One full frame and three more.
Find the number: thirteen.

18
00:01:11.117 --> 00:01:13.903
Now write fourteen. Press one, then four.

19
00:01:14.123 --> 00:01:18.303
The one means one whole ten.
The four is the four loose ones.

20
00:01:18.803 --> 00:01:22.100
Count in tens with whole
frames. Ten. Twenty.

21
00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:26.685
Socks come in pairs. Count
in twos: two, four, six.

22
00:01:26.905 --> 00:01:31.085
Now pair them up. Six makes three
pairs, with nobody left over.

23
00:01:31.305 --> 00:01:35.299
Six is even. Seven has one
left alone, so seven is odd.

24
00:01:35.519 --> 00:01:39.512
Even, odd, even, odd. They take
turns all the way to twenty.

25
00:01:40.012 --> 00:01:43.403
Which is bigger, nine or
fifteen? The number line knows.

26
00:01:43.623 --> 00:01:47.198
Fifteen is further to the
right, so fifteen is bigger.

27
00:01:47.418 --> 00:01:51.319
Put them in order. Smallest
first: four, nine, fifteen.

28
00:01:51.539 --> 00:01:55.115
First, second and third
tell you a place in a line.

29
00:01:55.335 --> 00:01:59.283
Count from the front. The runner
nearest the flag is first.

30
00:01:59.783 --> 00:02:04.752
You can count to twenty, one number
for each thing. Zero means none.

31
00:02:04.972 --> 00:02:09.291
Ten and some more makes thirteen.
You can count in twos and tens.

32
00:02:09.511 --> 00:02:13.086
Pairs show odd and even,
with nobody left over.

33
00:02:13.306 --> 00:02:16.743
And first, second, third
say where you are in a line.
